<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Chronic rhinosinusitis without nasal polyps (J32.9)</content>. Persistent symptoms after appropriate medical therapy: ten weeks of high-volume saline irrigation and an intranasal steroid with a culture-directed antibiotic course, with a SNOT-22 of 41. The CT shows mucosal thickening filling most of the left maxillary sinus and the left anterior ethmoid cells, partial opacification of the left frontal sinus, and a blocked left ostiomeatal complex, with mild thickening of the right maxillary and anterior ethmoid sinuses; Lund-Mackay score 9 (left 7, right 2). No bone erosion, no sign of a fungal ball, no dental source, and no thinning of the skull base or the orbital wall. She meets the appropriateness criteria for endoscopic sinus surgery. Dr. Lindgren discussed the expected benefit, the risks (bleeding, scarring, rarely injury to the eye or skull base and a leak of spinal fluid), and the alternative of continuing medical treatment. She chose surgery. [S]<list><item>Left endoscopic sinus surgery in about two weeks: maxillary antrostomy, anterior ethmoidectomy, frontal recess dissection.</item><item>Continue saline irrigation and fluticasone until surgery; after surgery budesonide in the saline rinse replaces the spray.</item><item>No aspirin or anti-inflammatory painkillers for a week before surgery; acetaminophen for pain afterwards.</item><item>No flying after surgery until Dr. Lindgren clears her.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Left middle ear barotrauma (T70.0XXA)</content>. Resolved: the drum is normal and her hearing has recovered. [S]<list><item>Ear clearing on every descent; never fly while blocked.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Protocol monitoring: items the monitoring protocol calls for at this visit. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Domain</th><th>Measure</th><th>Interval</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Survey</td><td>SNOT-22 total score (0 to 110; 20 or more after medical therapy supports surgery)</td><td>At diagnosis, after each treatment step, and after surgery</td><td>Completed today</td><td>41, from 52 at the first visit and 44 at four weeks; still 20 or more after appropriate medical therapy</td></tr><tr><td>Survey</td><td>Facial pain or pressure score (0 to 10)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>4 of 10, from 7</td></tr><tr><td>Imaging</td><td>CT of the paranasal sinuses (Lund-Mackay score, anatomy, red flags)</td><td>After appropriate medical therapy when surgery is being considered, or at once for complications</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Lund-Mackay 9 (left 7, right 2); blocked left ostiomeatal complex; no erosion or dental source</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Endoscopically guided culture of pus</td><td>When pus is seen and an antibiotic is considered</td><td>Up to date</td><td>The flare culture grew beta-lactamase-producing Haemophilus influenzae, sensitive to amoxicillin-clavulanate; no pus since</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Antibiotic for an acute exacerbation</td><td>Only for an acute exacerbation, guided by culture where possible</td><td>Deferred</td><td>The phone check planned for the end of the antibiotic course was not made, because it was not booked as a task when the antibiotic was ordered. No harm resulted: she was better at the preventive visit five days later, and the pus has not returned. Corrective action: the clinic now books the check as a task at the time of the order</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Nasal endoscopy (polyps, edema, pus, crusting, healing)</td><td>To confirm the diagnosis, at flares, and after surgery</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Endoscopy at diagnosis; next in the otolaryngology clinic after surgery</td></tr><tr><td>Examination</td><td>Warning signs of complications (eye swelling, vision change, severe headache, stiff neck, confusion)</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>None</td></tr><tr><td>Laboratory</td><td>Conditions that change management (asthma, cystic fibrosis, immune deficiency, ciliary dyskinesia, aspirin sensitivity, dental source)</td><td>At diagnosis, and if disease does not respond</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Dental check normal; no dental source on CT; no asthma</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Saline irrigation: daily use, technique, and safe water</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Twice daily for ten weeks; technique correct</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Intranasal steroid: daily use, technique, nosebleeds</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Daily for ten weeks; no nosebleeds</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Oral corticosteroid</td><td>Short course for disease with polyps; not routine without polyps</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>No polyps</td></tr><tr><td>Medication safety</td><td>Medicines acceptable for pilots and the 48-hour ground trial of a new medicine</td><td>With every new medicine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>No new medicine today; the budesonide rinse after surgery is a nasal corticosteroid, acceptable for pilots</td></tr><tr><td>Self-management</td><td>Fitness to fly: not flying while blocked or in pain (14 CFR 61.53); sinus and ear clearing on descent</td><td>Every visit</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Ear recovered; still blocked, so not flying; no flying after surgery until cleared</td></tr><tr><td>Referral</td><td>Endoscopic sinus surgery evaluation (appropriateness criteria and shared decision)</td><td>When symptoms persist after appropriate medical therapy with disease on CT</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Criteria met (Lund-Mackay 1 or more; nasal steroid plus a culture-directed antibiotic; SNOT-22 20 or more); she chose surgery after a shared decision with Dr. Lindgren</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Goal progress:</paragraph><list><item>SNOT-22 total score below 20 by four months. In progress; target 2026-10-12 [S]</item><item>Fly without sinus or ear pressure pain, and only when she meets the FAA rule of not flying while symptomatic, by four months. In progress; target 2026-10-12 [S]</item><item>Use only medicines acceptable for pilots, with a 48-hour ground trial of each new medicine and no sedating antihistamine. In progress; target 2026-10-12 [S]</item><item>Saline irrigation and the nasal steroid used daily with correct technique. Outcome by 2026-08-17: Daily use with correct technique at ten weeks; no nosebleeds [S]</item></list><paragraph>Guideline basis: Clinical practice guideline (update): adult sinusitis (2015); International consensus statement on allergy and rhinology: rhinosinusitis 2021 (2021); Defining appropriateness criteria for endoscopic sinus surgery during management of uncomplicated adult chronic rhinosinusitis: a RAND/UCLA appropriateness study (2016); Is there a role for antibiotics in the treatment of chronic rhinosinusitis? (review of guideline recommendations, including ICAR 2021) (2022); Guide for Aviation Medical Examiners: Over-the-Counter (OTC) Medications Reference Guide, What OTC Medications Can I Take and Still Be Safe to Fly? (2024); Guide for Aviation Medical Examiners: Allergy - Antihistamine and Immunotherapy Medication (2026); Ear Barotrauma (StatPearls) (2023)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Endoscopic sinus surgery in about two weeks, with cleaning in the otolaryngology clinic one and three weeks afterwards. Review here about five weeks after surgery. Next scheduled visit 2026-10-12: review five weeks after endoscopic sinus surgery: symptoms, healing, pathology, and return to flying. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
